title: By intent or by habit?
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

Shape and form chosen for more imperfect wood to be used AND then to showcase those imperfections. Chairs made in a more traditional manner would be made "by a craftsperson" and the goal would be to showcase the craft, so you would prefer to use "better" stock. I think imperfect stock, well, it could be used for other smaller projects, it could be used for more [[vernacular|Vernacular]], functional, practical furniture or shop tools...

Yes, for this chair, yes, flaws are to be shown. A traditional woodworked chair would try to work around that, or perhaps hide the flaws on the underside of a seat, as interior parts of drawers, etc.

Maybe it's more about "salvaged wood," or wood that in some hands, some contexts, would be seen as undesirable... but that seems to be generally changing. Although I can't see the makers of a Finn Juhl chair, nor [[Hans Wegner]]'s [[Wishbone Chair]] makers, wanting to use wood with these qualities.
